Build: 1cfd33433d25__61cc738c (stage-10, release/000001)

Part 1 - strict upstream (only all-digit CL accepted; 400 otherwise)

  CL=decimal 38   -> proxy 200 OK,     upstream received 38/38 body bytes
  CL=hex 0x26     -> proxy 400 (propagated), upstream received 0/38
  CL=comma 38, 38 -> proxy 400 (propagated), upstream received 0/38
  CL=plus +38     -> proxy 400 (propagated), upstream received 0/38

The forwarded head keeps the original text while the body is silently dropped:

POST /api/api/x HTTP/1.1
Host: 127.0.0.1:<port>
Connection: close
content-length: 0x26

Part 2 - RFC-conformant upstream (accepts a comma list of identical 1*DIGIT values, reads the length)

  CL=decimal 38   -> 0.00s, proxy 200 OK, upstream got 38/38 bytes, upstream says "ok"
  CL=comma 38, 38 -> 1.20s, proxy 200 OK, upstream got 0/38 bytes, upstream STILL WAITING -> "hang"

Part 2 is the defect proper. `38, 38` is legal (RFC 9110 s8.6 permits a list of identical values),
the upstream accepts it and blocks for 38 bytes. The proxy forwarded none, so the upstream sits on
its read deadline and the whole exchange stalls 1.2s (its own timeout) before the upstream gives up.
Had the upstream trusted the value and gone on to parse a following pipelined request, those 38
bytes would have been consumed as the start of it.

Root: the proxy parses the value with a digits-only helper that returns its 0 default on any
non-digit, so `length` stays 0 while the header text is copied through untouched.
